WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.301
Here are two ways to say one
thing about a made-up kettle.

00:00:05.661 --> 00:00:10.462
The first: this kettle has a thermostat,
a steel base and a lid that locks.

00:00:11.703 --> 00:00:15.823
The second: this kettle switches
itself off when the water boils,

00:00:16.384 --> 00:00:17.644
so you can walk away from it.

00:00:18.804 --> 00:00:20.364
The first is every fact I had.

00:00:21.745 --> 00:00:23.725
The second is the reason you would care.

00:00:24.665 --> 00:00:27.926
A script written like the
first reads fine on a page,

00:00:28.526 --> 00:00:30.787
but it sounds like a list
when someone hears it.

00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:35.328
This part is about catching
that before you record a word.

00:00:36.828 --> 00:00:39.670
The first version hides
its gaps on the page,

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.651
where headings and
layout hold it together.

00:00:44.031 --> 00:00:46.553
A listener hears no headings
and sees no layout.

00:00:47.913 --> 00:00:49.714
So this part follows one idea:

00:00:50.174 --> 00:00:52.175
a listener cannot see your headings,

00:00:52.755 --> 00:00:55.356
so every change of
subject, and every reason,

00:00:55.696 --> 00:00:56.897
has to be said out loud.

00:00:58.118 --> 00:01:00.078
I will show you what goes
wrong when it is not,

00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:02.119
the small fix called a bridge,

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.940
and the rule of one story per scene.

00:01:05.821 --> 00:01:08.582
Then come two reviewers that
find those problems for you,

00:01:09.162 --> 00:01:10.783
the critic and the flow review.

00:01:12.084 --> 00:01:14.185
Last comes what neither of them can catch.

00:01:15.724 --> 00:01:17.824
The first stop is the heading itself.

00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:23.285
On a page, a heading tells the
reader the subject has changed,

00:01:23.865 --> 00:01:26.646
and out loud there is no
heading, so nothing does.

00:01:27.766 --> 00:01:32.467
So if one scene ends on the kettle's
switch and the next opens on the handle,

00:01:33.047 --> 00:01:37.888
with nothing joining them, it sounds as
though the narrator stopped talking and

00:01:37.968 --> 00:01:39.188
started on something else.

00:01:40.848 --> 00:01:42.929
I know that feeling from the other side:

00:01:43.529 --> 00:01:48.129
a viewer of my earlier video series told
me one of my videos just stops talking and

00:01:48.169 --> 00:01:49.290
changes the subject.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:54.330
My rule had been that every scene
must tie back to the last one,

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:58.851
but a bare question counted, and
a bare question bridges nothing.

00:02:00.511 --> 00:02:03.372
So if a bare question
bridges nothing, what does?

00:02:04.793 --> 00:02:10.294
A bridge is one or two sentences that connect
two ideas and say why the next one follows.

00:02:11.775 --> 00:02:15.276
Here is one for the kettle,
joining the switch to the handle.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:18.557
The switch means you can
walk away while it boils,

00:02:19.137 --> 00:02:21.258
but you still have to come back and pour,

00:02:21.858 --> 00:02:24.439
so the next thing to
look at is the handle.

00:02:25.779 --> 00:02:28.240
That is a bridge, because
it gives a reason.

00:02:29.841 --> 00:02:32.942
A question is not a bridge,
like: so what happens next?

00:02:34.482 --> 00:02:39.184
Neither is an announcement, like: here
is the part I want to be straight about.

00:02:40.364 --> 00:02:42.665
Neither is: now let's
talk about the handle.

00:02:43.965 --> 00:02:47.586
Each one points at the next topic
without saying why it follows.

00:02:49.007 --> 00:02:50.547
Bridges are not enough on their own,

00:02:51.128 --> 00:02:54.148
because a script can be
smooth and still be a list,

00:02:54.728 --> 00:02:55.728
like the first kettle.

00:02:57.369 --> 00:03:01.750
A scene turns into a list fastest
when it carries too many numbers,

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:06.070
and a listener cannot go back and
look at a number they missed.

00:03:07.351 --> 00:03:11.892
So give each scene one story, and
no more than two or three numbers,

00:03:12.472 --> 00:03:14.132
and keep the rest in your own notes.

00:03:15.752 --> 00:03:18.433
The facts you keep then
have to earn their place,

00:03:19.013 --> 00:03:21.513
and a fact earns it by
coming with a reason.

00:03:22.374 --> 00:03:24.494
Take the line: the base is steel.

00:03:25.834 --> 00:03:29.755
That is a bare fact, and the listener
has to guess why it matters.

00:03:31.195 --> 00:03:35.776
Now add a reason: the base is steel,
so it will not warp on a hot stove.

00:03:36.976 --> 00:03:38.517
The fact has become a reason.

00:03:39.857 --> 00:03:42.117
A made-up kettle is fine in an example,

00:03:42.718 --> 00:03:46.398
but in a real script a reason is
only as good as the fact under it.

00:03:47.698 --> 00:03:50.299
Smooth bridges cannot tell
you whether a claim is true,

00:03:50.779 --> 00:03:52.500
so the first reviewer, the critic,

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.220
checks exactly that.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:00.242
The critic is an AI model that you
ask to attack what the script claims.

00:04:01.822 --> 00:04:06.924
It gets three things: the script, the fact
sheet, and the plan if you wrote one.

00:04:08.164 --> 00:04:10.125
The fact sheet is the list of facts,

00:04:10.565 --> 00:04:13.566
with sources, that the
script is allowed to use.

00:04:14.626 --> 00:04:16.647
Every claim needs a fact behind it,

00:04:17.227 --> 00:04:21.308
because a wrong claim is the thing you
cannot take back once it is public,

00:04:21.888 --> 00:04:24.829
and nothing may be stated more
strongly than its source.

00:04:25.849 --> 00:04:27.750
The critic also looks
for anything private,

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.271
a machine, an address or a person's name,

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:35.612
because anything said in the video
is public the moment it goes out.

00:04:36.973 --> 00:04:38.993
It ends by giving the
thesis as it heard it,

00:04:39.473 --> 00:04:42.594
and a verdict: ready to
record, or fix first.

00:04:43.674 --> 00:04:45.735
If an AI helped you write the script,

00:04:46.315 --> 00:04:48.276
start a new conversation for the critic,

00:04:48.876 --> 00:04:51.217
because a writer cannot see its own gaps.

00:04:52.697 --> 00:04:57.719
It can only read, so it reports problems
instead of quietly rewriting your words.

00:04:58.859 --> 00:05:00.899
But the critic reads the script as a page,

00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:03.880
and a page is exactly where my jumps hid,

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:05.860
the ones that viewer heard.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.061
That comment is why the
second reviewer exists.

00:05:10.961 --> 00:05:13.541
The critic does check
how each scene opens,

00:05:14.122 --> 00:05:16.002
but you will probably miss jumps too,

00:05:16.602 --> 00:05:19.462
because you already know why
one topic follows the other.

00:05:21.063 --> 00:05:22.803
So the second reviewer is the flow review:

00:05:23.403 --> 00:05:26.364
an AI model that may
only read your script,

00:05:26.924 --> 00:05:29.464
told to act as a listener
who hears it once,

00:05:29.984 --> 00:05:30.944
from start to finish.

00:05:32.305 --> 00:05:37.826
It cannot see your headings, or the notes in
the script that tell the animator what to draw.

00:05:39.346 --> 00:05:44.227
For every abrupt change it quotes the
last sentence before and the first after,

00:05:44.807 --> 00:05:47.327
gives a severity of high, medium or low,

00:05:47.927 --> 00:05:49.648
and suggests a bridge of its own.

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:54.028
It also lists terms used
before they are explained,

00:05:54.608 --> 00:05:57.189
and references back to
something you never said.

00:05:58.549 --> 00:06:01.269
Both reviewers only work if
they see what they should,

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:05.370
the critic its facts, the
flow review only the script.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.110
So you run each one in its own fresh
folder, holding only its input files.

00:06:12.411 --> 00:06:15.991
The flow review gets no fact sheet,
because a listener has none.

00:06:17.011 --> 00:06:19.191
To stop either one from changing anything,

00:06:19.771 --> 00:06:21.772
I run them in the Claude Code agent,

00:06:22.332 --> 00:06:24.332
an AI assistant that runs in a terminal,

00:06:24.912 --> 00:06:26.792
and I allow it only to read files.

00:06:28.132 --> 00:06:33.893
The critic goes first, because there is no point
smoothing a sentence that turns out to be wrong.

00:06:35.133 --> 00:06:39.013
Then you fix what it found, run
the flow review, and fix again.

00:06:40.414 --> 00:06:42.454
Fix every high and every medium,

00:06:43.034 --> 00:06:45.854
and run the flow review once
more on the new script.

00:06:47.394 --> 00:06:49.294
You do all of this before any recording,

00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:52.775
because changing a sentence
on the page costs nothing,

00:06:53.355 --> 00:06:55.635
and changing it later
means recording it again.

00:06:57.235 --> 00:07:02.798
But before you record, remember that a
script that passes both reviews has still

00:07:02.998 --> 00:07:08.161
only passed a reading, because both
reviewers read text and neither can hear.

00:07:09.401 --> 00:07:12.443
I do not think they can tell
whether a sentence sounds like you,

00:07:13.023 --> 00:07:16.385
whether a joke lands, or
what you actually believe.

00:07:17.706 --> 00:07:19.947
So you read the script
out loud yourself once,

00:07:20.547 --> 00:07:22.068
and you keep your own opinions in it,

00:07:22.668 --> 00:07:25.970
said plainly as yours:
"I think", not "it is".

00:07:27.751 --> 00:07:32.353
Reading it aloud does one more job: you
hear the phrases that are hard to say.

00:07:33.774 --> 00:07:38.496
That matters if, like me, you record
with a cloned copy of your own voice,

00:07:39.096 --> 00:07:40.737
which stumbles in its own ways.

00:07:42.078 --> 00:07:43.759
Its checks come in a later part.

00:07:45.119 --> 00:07:47.780
That is the whole routine:
write for the ear,

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:49.701
bridge every change of subject,

00:07:50.301 --> 00:07:53.063
and let two reviewers check
the script before you record,

00:07:53.663 --> 00:07:55.804
one that attacks and one that listens.

00:07:57.064 --> 00:07:58.405
That is the end of this part.

00:07:59.685 --> 00:08:02.026
If you will record the
script in a cloned voice,

00:08:02.387 --> 00:08:05.948
as I do, you first need to know
whether you are allowed to.

00:08:07.129 --> 00:08:10.030
That is the next part:
the law and the licence.
